member.summary:
A phonemic transcription of the element's text content (ISO 32000-2 §14.9.6). The value is a text string representing the pronunciation using the phonetic alphabet specified by .
New in PDF 2.0. When set, the phoneme is serialized as the /Phoneme entry on the structure element dictionary. Screen readers use this value to produce correct speech output for text that might otherwise be mispronounced (e.g., proper names, technical terms, abbreviations, foreign words).
var span = paragraph.AddChild(StructureType.Span); span.Phoneme = "ˈniːtʃə"; span.PhoneticAlphabet = PdfPhoneticAlphabet.Ipa;

             Provides access to bundled open-source fonts (SIL Open Font License) that ship
             with ObviousPDF as embedded resources. These fonts can be used as drop-in
             replacements for the standard 14 PDF fonts, ensuring consistent rendering
             across all PDF viewers without relying on system-installed fonts.


Font Families:


Serif — CMU Serif (Computer Modern Unicode Serif): a Times/Times New Roman substitute.

Sans — Sora: a modern geometric sans-serif, substitute for Helvetica/Arial.

Mono — CMU Typewriter Text: a monospace font, substitute for Courier.




             All bundled fonts are licensed under the SIL Open Font License v1.1 and may be
             freely embedded, bundled, and redistributed with any software.




             // Use a bundled font directly
             var options = new PdfTextOptions { EmbeddedFont = BundledFonts.SerifRegular, FontSize = 12 };
             page.AddText("Hello with embedded serif!", 72, 720, options);

             // Get the embedded substitute for a standard font
             var font = BundledFonts.GetSubstitute(StandardFont.Helvetica);
             var options2 = new PdfTextOptions { EmbeddedFont = font, FontSize = 14 };
             page.AddText("Hello with embedded sans!", 72, 700, options2);

Represents an associated file that can be attached to a PDF document,
page, or structure element (ISO 32000-2 §14.13).



Associated files provide a mechanism for associating related content
with PDF objects. Each associated file includes an embedded file stream
(§7.11.4) wrapped in a file specification dictionary (§7.11.3) with an
/AFRelationship entry that describes the nature of the association.




The file specification is added to an /AF array on the target
object (catalog for document-level, page dictionary for page-level, or
structure element dictionary for element-level associations).




// Document-level associated file
var xmlData = File.ReadAllBytes("source.xml");
var af = new PdfAssociatedFile("source.xml", "application/xml", xmlData)
{
    Relationship = PdfAssociatedFileRelationship.Source,
    Description = "Original XML source data"
};
doc.AddAssociatedFile(af);

Represents the configuration for digitally signing a PDF document
(ISO 32000 §12.8). A digital signature cryptographically binds a
signer's identity to the document content, enabling detection of
any modifications after signing.



The signature uses the adbe.pkcs7.detached SubFilter
(§12.8.3.3) with the Adobe.PPKLite filter, producing a
PKCS#7 detached signature (RFC 2315 / RFC 5652) that covers
the entire PDF file except the signature's own /Contents value.




var cert = new X509Certificate2("certificate.pfx", "password");
var sig = new PdfDigitalSignature(cert)
{
    Reason = "Document approval",
    Location = "New York",
    ContactInfo = "john@example.com"
};
var doc = new PdfDocument();
var page = doc.AddPage();
page.AddText("Signed Document", 72, 720);
doc.Sign("output.pdf", sig);

Represents a PDF document that can be built incrementally and saved to a file or stream.
This is the main entry point for creating PDF files with ObviousPDF.



The document follows the structure defined in ISO 32000 §7.7:
Catalog → Page Tree → Pages → Content Streams.




var doc = new PdfDocument();
doc.Info.Title = "My Document";
var page = doc.AddPage();
page.AddText("Hello, World!", 72, 720);
doc.Save("output.pdf");

member.param:
Zero-based index at which to insert the first source page.
Pass 0 to prepend before all existing pages;
pass  to append after all existing pages.

member.summary:
Represents a node in the document part hierarchy (ISO 32000-2 §14.12).



Document parts provide a hierarchical structure for organizing pages
of a PDF document into logical groups. This is primarily used in
variable-data printing (VDP) workflows where a single PDF contains
multiple logical documents (e.g., individual letters, invoices, or
statements), each consisting of one or more contiguous pages.




The hierarchy forms a tree: intermediate nodes contain child
 nodes via ,
while leaf nodes reference a contiguous range of pages via
 and .




Each node may carry optional metadata via 
(DPM dictionary, §14.12). Metadata is represented as a flat
dictionary of string key-value pairs serialized as a PDF dictionary.




var doc = new PdfDocument();
// Add pages
for (int i = 0; i < 6; i++)
    doc.AddPage().AddText($"Page {i + 1}", 72, 720);

// Create document part hierarchy
var root = doc.CreateDocumentPartRoot();
var letter1 = root.AddChild(0, 2); // pages 0-2
letter1.Metadata["Recipient"] = "Alice";
var letter2 = root.AddChild(3, 5); // pages 3-5
letter2.Metadata["Recipient"] = "Bob";

Represents a TrueType or OpenType font loaded from a .ttf/.otf file
that can be embedded in a PDF document (ISO 32000 §9.6.3, §9.8, §9.9).



Unlike the standard 14 PDF fonts, embedded fonts allow any typeface
and any character that exists in the font to be used. The font program
is subset to include only the glyphs actually used in the document,
keeping file size small.




var font = PdfEmbeddedFont.FromFile("Roboto-Regular.ttf");
var options = new PdfTextOptions { EmbeddedFont = font, FontSize = 14 };
page.AddText("Hello with a custom font!", 72, 720, options);

example.code:
var font = PdfEmbeddedFont.FromFile("Roboto-Regular.ttf");
double width = font.MeasureTextWidth("Hello World", 12);
// width ≈ 60.5 (depends on the font's metrics)

member.summary:
             Represents the encryption configuration for a PDF document
             (ISO 32000 §7.6). When applied to a document, all strings and
             streams are encrypted using the specified algorithm and passwords.



             PDF encryption uses two passwords:


UserPassword — required to open the document. An empty
               string allows anyone to open it (but permissions still apply).

OwnerPassword — grants full access, overriding all
               permission restrictions.





             var doc = new PdfDocument();
             doc.Encryption = new PdfEncryption
             {
                 UserPassword = "",              // Anyone can open
                 OwnerPassword = "secret",       // Full access with this password
                 AllowPrinting = true,
                 AllowCopying = false
             };
             var page = doc.AddPage();
             page.AddText("Encrypted content", 72, 720);
             doc.Save("encrypted.pdf");

Represents an Optional Content Group (OCG) as defined in ISO 32000 §8.11.2.
An OCG is a named layer of content that can be selectively shown or hidden
by a PDF viewer. OCGs are referenced by the /OC entry in marked
content sequences (BDC/EMC) and XObject dictionaries.



Each OCG becomes an indirect object with /Type /OCG and a /Name
entry. OCGs are collected in the document's Optional Content Properties
dictionary (/OCProperties in the Catalog, §8.11.4).




var doc = new PdfDocument();
var watermark = doc.CreateOptionalContentGroup("Watermark");
var annotations = doc.CreateOptionalContentGroup("Annotations", visible: false);
var page = doc.AddPage();
page.BeginOptionalContent(watermark);
page.AddText("DRAFT", 200, 400);
page.EndOptionalContent();
doc.Save("layers.pdf");

Applies a rotation transformation to the CTM (§8.3.4). Rotates counter-clockwise around the origin by the specified angle.
To rotate around a specific point (cx, cy), use:
page.SaveGraphicsState() .Translate(cx, cy) .Rotate(angleDegrees) .Translate(-cx, -cy) // ... draw content ... .RestoreGraphicsState();

Adds a text (sticky note) annotation to the page (§12.5.6.4).
The annotation appears as an icon at the specified position;
clicking it opens a pop-up note with the given contents.

example.code:
var page = doc.AddPage();
var opts = new PdfTextOptions { FontSize = 14, Font = StandardFont.Helvetica };
double width = page.MeasureTextWidth("Hello World", opts);
